.TH HOTKEY 1
.SH NAME
hotkey \- run a program with its keys filtered
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hotkey
[
.B -D
] [
.B -f
.I rules
] [
.B -s
.I srvname
]
.I cmd
[
.I arg ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Hotkey
runs
.I cmd
in a new name space with its own
.B /dev/cons
and
.BR /dev/consctl ,
and reads the keyboard from the original
.BR /dev/cons .
When the keys typed match a rule, the rule's command runs and
.I cmd
does not see those keys.
Other keys pass through.
If a sequence of keys starts a rule but does not finish one,
.I hotkey
discards its keys.
If
.IR hotkey 's
standard input is
.BR /dev/cons ,
.IR cmd 's
is the filtered keyboard.
.PP
.I Hotkey
affects only programs that read keys from
.BR /dev/cons ,
as
.IR keyboard (2)
does.
Under
.IR rio (1),
a program in a window gets only the keys typed in that window;
.I hotkey
run on
.I rio
itself gets every key, before
.I rio
does.
In cooked mode keys arrive a line at a time,
and
.I hotkey
matches them as they arrive.
.PP
.I Hotkey
reads the rules from
.IR rules ,
or else from
.BI $home/lib/hotkey/ name
or
.BI /lib/hotkey/ name\f1,
whichever exists first, where
.I name
is the last element of
.IR cmd .
With no rules file there are no rules.
Each rule is one line: keys separated by blanks, then an
.IR rc (1)
command in braces that runs to the end of the line.
Lines starting with
.B #
are comments.
A line
.BI include " file"
reads the rules in
.IR file ,
or in
.BI /lib/hotkey/ file
if its name does not start with
.BR / .
A key is a character;
.BI cmd- c\f1,
for the Mac's Command key with
.IR c ;
.BI ctl- c\f1,
for Control with
.IR c ,
one of
.B @a-z[\e]^_\f1;
.B f1
to
.BR f12 ;
or one of
.BR bs ,
.BR del ,
.BR down ,
.BR end ,
.BR esc ,
.BR home ,
.BR ins ,
.BR left ,
.BR nl ,
.BR pgdown ,
.BR pgup ,
.BR print ,
.BR right ,
.BR space ,
.BR tab ,
and
.BR up .
A rule replaces any earlier rule that clashes with it:
.B ctl-x ctl-s
replaces an earlier
.BR "ctl-x ctl-s" ,
.BR ctl-x ,
or
.BR "ctl-x ctl-s q" .
So a rule after an
.B include
can change a key the included file binds.
.PP
A line
.BI mode " name"
starts a section of rules for the mode
.IR name ,
which apply only while that mode is on;
a line
.B mode
alone goes back to the rules for no mode.
Sections with the same name are one mode.
A section lasts to the next
.B mode
line or the end of its file:
an included file's rules start in the including file's section,
and after the
.B include
the including file goes on in the section it was in.
.PP
While a mode is on,
.I hotkey
matches keys against its rules alone.
A rule's command runs and the mode stays on.
A key the mode does not bind turns the mode off and is then handled as with no mode on:
it passes through, or starts or finishes a rule for no mode.
A key that breaks off a sequence of the mode's keys turns the mode off too,
and is discarded with them, as with no mode on.
Keys typed by
.B send
are matched in the same way,
so a mode's rule that sends a key the mode does not bind ends the mode.
.PP
Commands run in
.IR hotkey 's
name space and environment, with standard input
.BR /dev/null ,
while
.I hotkey
goes on reading keys.
.PP
.I Hotkey
serves these files at
.BR /mnt/hotkey ,
and with
.B -s
posts them at
.BI /srv/ srvname\f1:
.TF consctl
.TP
.B cons
.IR cmd 's
keyboard.
.TP
.B consctl
As in
.IR cons (3).
.I Hotkey
passes
.B rawon
and
.B rawoff
to the real
.BR consctl ,
and turns raw mode off when the file descriptor that set it closes.
.TP
.B mode
The mode.
Writing a mode's name turns it on, in place of any mode that is on;
a name no section has fails the write.
The mode lasts while the file descriptor that turned it on is open,
and ends when it closes, or when it writes an empty line,
or at a key the mode does not bind, as above.
A mode another descriptor replaced does not come back when that one closes.
A rule's command can turn a mode on for as long as it holds the file, as
.B "{echo m; sleep 2} >/mnt/hotkey/mode"
does for two seconds.
Replacing the rules keeps the mode on if the new rules have it, and otherwise turns it off.
Reading gives the name of the mode that is on, or nothing.
.TP
.B rules
The rules. Closing the file after writing replaces them.
.I Hotkey
checks each line as it completes; an error fails that write
and keeps the old rules.
It checks a last line with no newline when the file closes;
an error there goes to standard error and keeps the old rules.
Opening the file without truncating it and writing nothing
leaves the rules as they are.
.TP
.B send
Writing key names, separated by blanks, types those keys.
An unknown name fails the write and types nothing.
.PD
.PP
The
.B -D
option prints 9P messages.
.I Hotkey
exits when
.I cmd
does, with its status.
.SH EXAMPLES
Save the focused Acme window with Command-S, or with Control-X Control-S as in Emacs.
.I Acmestart
runs
.IR acme (1)
under
.I hotkey
and sets
.B $acmewin
to the focused window and
.B $acmesrv
to the acme's
.B /srv
file.
Acme's files are not in
.IR hotkey 's
name space, so a rule mounts them:
.IP
.EX
cmd-s	{test -e /mnt/acme/index || mount $acmesrv /mnt/acme; echo put >/mnt/acme/$acmewin/ctl}
ctl-x ctl-s	{test -e /mnt/acme/index || mount $acmesrv /mnt/acme; echo put >/mnt/acme/$acmewin/ctl}
.EE
.PP
Switch to another set of rules:
.IP
.EX
cmd-g	{cat $home/lib/hotkey/acme.g >/mnt/hotkey/rules}
.EE
.PP
Number the choices in a window, as
.BR /lib/hotkey/acme 's
rules do for
.IR Act 's
.BR +Act :
Command-. runs
.IR Act ,
which holds
.B mode
open with
.B act
written to it while its window lasts,
and 1 to 9 and Esc go to the pipe it posted:
.IP
.EX
cmd-.	{/acme/bin/akey Act -m}
mode act
1	{test -e /srv/act.$user && echo 1 >>/srv/act.$user}
esc	{test -e /srv/act.$user && echo esc >>/srv/act.$user}
.EE
.PP
Make Control-A go to the start of the line in a program that only knows Home:
.IP
.EX
ctl-a	{echo home >/mnt/hotkey/send}
.EE
.SH FILES
.TF /lib/hotkey/*
.TP
.B /lib/hotkey/*
system rules, by program
.TP
.B $home/lib/hotkey/*
user rules
.TP
.B /lib/hotkey/rio
the Mac's Command keys as control keys:
Command-C, V, X and Z become control-C, V, X and Z,
and Command-Shift-Z becomes control-R
.TP
.B /lib/hotkey/acme
Acme's keys, with the mode
.B act
for
.IR Act 's
window (see
.IR alsp (4))
.TP
.B /lib/hotkey/acme.emacs
Emacs's keys for
.IR acme ;
.B include acme.emacs
in
.B $home/lib/hotkey/acme
uses them
.PD
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/hotkey.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR cons (3),
.IR keyboard (2),
.IR rio (1),
.IR plumber (4)
.SH BUGS
.I Hotkey
matches keys typed by
.B send
against the rules, so a rule that sends its own keys runs forever.
.br
Rules cannot name keys that send nothing, such as Alt alone.
